1. Verbatim quote:

> “46 rows × every realized key, so a ledger error in ANY route would surface as a per-key integer mismatch.”

Classification: MINOR.

This overstates the finite battery. Its roster has \(N\le 9\), so it realizes only bounded depths. For example:

- \(\alpha(k=3)\) first exists when \(N\ge10\);
- \(\beta_2(k=3)\) requires \(u_0>9\), hence \(N\ge11\);
- \(\beta_3(t=3)\) requires \(u_0>9\) with matching parity, hence minimally \(u_0=11,\ N\ge12\).

A ledger error confined to any of those depths would produce no mismatch on the 46 sealed rows. The generic slot-count argument may still prove the formulas, but the machine leg cannot justify “ANY route error would surface.”

2. Verbatim quote:

> “a LAW violation on a NEW deep row with the committed-table rows clean refutes the transport lemmas (H-2/H-3) before the partition”

Classification: MINOR.

That diagnosis is not logically forced. A range, parity, or completeness defect in H-1 that first appears at an untested depth—such as omitting an \(\alpha(3)\) or \(\beta_3(t=3)\) locus—would leave every committed row clean and make a new deep LAW row fail. A LAW failure alone does not distinguish partition failure from transport failure; the accompanying species diagnostics would be needed.

3. Verbatim quote:

> “its fraction → 0 as \(N \to \infty\) per S5.3”

Classification: MINOR.

S5.3 supplies only
\[
u(N)\le q^{2N-2}\left(1+\frac{N}{2(q+1)}\right).
\]
This implies a lower bound
\[
\frac{q^{2N-2}}{u(N)}
 \ge \frac{1}{1+N/(2(q+1))},
\]
whose right side tends to zero; it does not prove that the fraction itself tends to zero. The claimed limit is nevertheless correct from the exact law: the positive \(k=1\) \(R\)-tail grows linearly in \(N\) relative to \(q^{2N-2}\). Thus this is a bad attribution, not a false asymptotic conclusion.

4. Verbatim quote:

> “So \(u(q,N)\) is one polynomial law in \((q,N)\)”

Classification: MINOR.

Literally, it is not polynomial jointly in \(q\) and \(N\): the formula contains \(\lfloor(M-1)/2\rfloor\), \(\lfloor(N-1)/3\rfloor\), and exponential terms \(q^N\). The earlier formulation—“a polynomial in \(q\) for each \(N\)”—is correct. This appears to be loose terminology in S5.4, with no effect on characteristic-independence.

